  "proof_review": [
    {
      "topic": "Square completion coverage",
      "finding": "The unknown cells are 1,2,16,17,18,19,27,35 and the unused symbols are 234789QY. A count depends only on which two cells hold Q and Y. Each of the 28 memberships represents exactly 2! times 6! = 1440 complete squares; membership is fixed globally per cell."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Loss geometry coverage",
      "finding": "Every five-loss witness has one allocation among 16 physical columns. The 15504 allocations partition the full C(170,5) mask space, with column lengths eleven for ten columns and ten for six columns. Each allocation fixes the retained source segment for every column."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Local coordinate-value reduction",
      "finding": "Once a pair is designated lost or retained, maximizing or minimizing its A-Z indicator has no effect on future source positions or costs. Keeping only an extremizing fill is exact. The chosen equal-count fill is ordered by transmitted coordinate position, including when that reverses the plaintext pair order."
    },
    {
      "topic": "State and dominance",
      "finding": "Only per-column used-loss counts are required. Their product is at most 2^5 = 32 states. Equal-state prefixes have identical future choices, so the better signed count dominates."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Canonical ties",
      "finding": "The increasing loss-position list is compared first, then its ADFGVX fill values. Equal-state histories contain equally many losses. Adding a common future mask and fill preserves this ordering even when future positions precede some past positions in ciphertext readout order."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Reporting",
      "finding": "The README distinguishes attainable extrema from attainment of every intermediate count. At least three digits is a necessary condition only within the stated family. The actual historical digit count is unknown, so this neither rejects nor authenticates the key."
    }
  ],
